{title: Come, Thou Fount of Every Blessing}
{artist: Robert Robinson (1735-1790)}
{key: D}
{copyright: Public Domain}

{sov: Verse 1}
[D]Come, Thou Fount of [A]every blessing, [G]tune my [A]heart to sing Thy [D]grace;
[D]Streams of mercy, [A]never ceasing, [G]call for [A]songs of loudest [D]praise.
[Bm]Teach me some me[G]lodious sonnet, [Bm]sung by [G]flaming tongues a[D]bove;
[D]Praise the mount! I’m [A]fixed upon it, [G]mount of [A]God’s unchanging [D]love.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 2}
[D]Here I raise my [A]Ebenezer; [G]hither [A]by Thy help I’m [D]come;
[D]And I hope, by [A]Thy good pleasure, [G]safely [A]to arrive at [D]home.
[Bm]Jesus sought me [G]when a stranger, [Bm]wandering [G]from the fold of [D]God;
[D]He, to rescue [A]me from danger, [G]inter[A]posed His precious [D]blood.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 3}
[D]O to grace how [A]great a debtor [G]daily [A]I’m constrained to [D]be!
[D]Let Thy goodness, [A]like a fetter, [G]bind my [A]wandering heart to [D]Thee.
[Bm]Prone to wander, [G]Lord, I feel it, [Bm]prone to [G]leave the God I [D]love;
[D]Here’s my heart, O [A]take and seal it, [G]seal it [A]for Thy courts a[D]bove.
{eov}
